{
  "identifier": "NG3929",
  "full_title": "Coriolanus persuaded by his Family to spare Rome",
  "short_title": "Coriolanus persuaded by his Family to spare Rome",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "high Renaissance",
  "material": "fresco, transferred to canvas",
  "dimensions": "125.7 × 125.7 cm",
  "production_date": "about 1509",
  "brief_description": "This picture, painted in fresco (directly onto wet plaster), was part of a series of eight which decorated the walls of the palace belonging to Pandolfo Petrucci, the ruler of Siena.  He commissioned the frescoes to celebrate the marriage of his son to Pope Pius III's niece. Two others survive in the National Gallery’s collection.\n\nCoriolanus, a high-ranking Roman general, was banished from Rome in 491 BC, taking refuge with the neighbouring Volsci. Some years later he led them to victory against Rome. His mother Volumnia and his wife Vergilia -- holding their baby -- are here shown leading a deputation of women to beg for his mercy and to spare the city. Coriolanus, overwhelmed by emotion, opens his arms ready to embrace his elder child. Eventually he withdrew his troops, choosing family over politics.\n\nThe patron, Pandolfo Petrucci, may have identified with such a scene: he was banished from Siena briefly in the 1490s and went into self-imposed exile in 1503.",
  "accession_date": "1924-01-01",
